   $q$How does the moment when Pip doubts her own light help develop the theme?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It shows that Pip is right, because her light turns out to be useless in the dark grass.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It explains why the clouds roll in and hide the moon for the rest of the night.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confuses-cause-effect"},{"text":"It shows that Pip acts even though she thinks her help is too small, which is the lesson of the fable.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It shows that Pip is jealous of the moon and wants to be brighter than it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows that Pip acts even though she thinks her help is too small, which is the lesson of the fable.$q$, $q$Pip asks "What good is a light like mine," yet she flies down anyway, and her small light works. Her light is not useless, since the beetle cries "I see you!"$q$, 'draft', '825b3092-fb82-5c21-bd93-b8164252b46a')
